# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern via `CancellationToken` is the modern replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block (like the original) but respect the timeout
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            // Unwrap single exceptions to match original behavior
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Async-preferred overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Pitfalls

| Issue | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running. |
| **Fire-and-forget leaks** | A timed-out but still-running task holds resources. Consider logging or tracking orphaned work. |
| **`default(T)` race** | The original had a subtle race—`result` could be read before fully assigned on some architectures. `Task.Run` eliminates this. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` gone** | Any `catch (ThreadAbortException)` blocks become dead code—remove them. |
| **Blocking callers** | If callers are already async, prefer `ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync` to avoid thread pool starvation from `.Wait()`. |

## Usage Comparison

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework)
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => SlowWork(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After (.NET 8) — work must check the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // check periodically
    return SlowWork(ct);
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

## If You Can't Make Work Cooperative

As a last resort, you can isolate uncooperative code in a separate **process** and kill it on timeout—this is the only safe forced-termination mechanism in .NET 8.
